    content: `Stand at a kiosk in the evening and look at the fridge before you buy anything. One row is water and soda in clear bottles. You can see the liquid, the bubbles, the label from the other side. The next row is juice, yoghurt drink, or cooking oil in a stiffer bottle you cannot read through. Same shop, same shelf, two different plastics.

The clear, crackly bottle is almost always PET — grade 1. It is light, it rustles when you squeeze it, and it springs back. The cloudy, stiffer bottle is almost always HDPE — grade 2. It feels like a milk jug or a detergent bottle. If you mix those two in one bag, a collector has to pull them apart again before anyone will buy the load.

Kenya already has people who live on this difference. A cart at the stage, a yard behind a hardware shop, a woman who buys only clear bottles on one day and only jerry cans on another — they are not guessing. They are buying one grade at a time because that is what a reprocessor pays for. Your job at home is the same job, done earlier, while the plastic is still clean.

Turn the bottle over. The small triangle with a number is the grade. Trust that mark more than the colour of the lid or the brand on the front. A juice bottle can look like a water bottle until you feel the wall. A water bottle can wear a sleeve that makes it look opaque. The triangle does not lie.

Keep a small PET pile and a small HDPE pile by the door. Caps off, rinsed, dry. When you photograph proof, put two or more items of the same grade in one frame. That is the habit the weekly board is built on: not a mixed sack, not a pretty pile of everything, one grade at a time.`,
