      "A cleanup that ends in one mixed mountain teaches the wrong lesson. Three sacks teach the one that lasts.",
    icon: "school",
    status: "ACTIVE",
    content: `Schools in Kenya already do cleanups. The picture is familiar: a line of pupils, a pile at the gate, a cheer, a photo. The pile is usually mixed. The next wind starts the lesson over. If the same school puts three sacks on the field — PET, HDPE, film — the hour teaches a trade, not just a tidy gate.

You do not need a speech. You need a table and a rule. Bottles here. Cans here. Bags here. Caps in a jar. Anything that is not those three waits in a fourth sack called residual. Pupils learn faster from the table than from a chart on a wall.

The leftover is the test. After the cheer, who owns the sacks? If they sit behind the kitchen until they smell, the lesson dies. If a known collector is told the day before, the sacks leave while they are still a product. That phone call is part of the cleanup. The photo of the pile is not.

If you are a parent in this app, you can run the same table at home in miniature. Children who have sorted at the door do not treat the school field as a dumping game. They treat it as a bigger kitchen. That is how a habit travels.

Do not turn a school hour into a promise the app cannot keep. No invented buyer, no invented amount, no claim that the school is now a factory. Three sacks, one collector, one afternoon. That is enough, and it is honest.`,
