    icon: "photo_camera",
    status: "ACTIVE",
    content: `Open the camera. Put two clean PET bottles in the frame, or two HDPE cans, or two PP cups. Daylight. No flash oil. No sack mouth hiding a mixed surprise. That is the picture. It looks too simple the first time. It is supposed to.

A mixed pretty pile is a common fail. People want to show effort. Effort that mixes grades is effort in the wrong direction. The reviewer is not scoring generosity. They are scoring whether the household can produce a product. Two items of one grade is a product sample. Ten items of four grades is a bin.

Fresh photo every time means the same bottles from last week do not come back in a new arrangement. The point of proof is that today’s habit happened. Reusing a picture is not a habit. It is a shortcut that burns trust for everyone on the board.

If the first photo is rejected, read the reason and shoot again the same day. Wet? Dry them. Mixed? Take the stranger out. Dark? Step to the door. The retry is part of the skill. Waiting for a new week because a photo was soft is how people drop the habit.

Stand where the reviewer can see the wall of the bottle, not only the label. The wall is how you tell PET from HDPE when the triangle is small on a phone. Your kitchen floor is fine. Your compound concrete is fine. A bouncing matatu seat is not.`,
