    title: "Film belongs in a tied sack",
    description:
      "Loose bags on a balcony are not stored. They are waiting for wind. A tied sack is the only film sort that survives a Kenyan afternoon.",
    icon: "work",
    status: "ACTIVE",
    content: `Film does not stack. It does not sit. It does not wait politely like a jerry can. If you leave wrappers in an open basin, the afternoon wind will file them in the neighbour’s plot. Then the neighbour is sorting your shopping, and they will not be grateful.

A tied sack is the whole technology. Not a clever bin. Not a poster. A sack you can close. Fill it with market bags, crisp packets, bread bags, the sleeve you peeled. When it is full, tie it, and start another. Keep it in a corner the wind cannot reach. That is containment, and containment is the sort.

Do not put bottles in that sack to “weigh it down.” You have just mixed PET into film. Do not put the film sack into the PET pile to “keep it together.” You have just mixed film into PET. The sack is a room. Only film lives in that room.

If nobody buys film on your street this month, the sack is still doing its job. It is not in the river. It is not in a cow. It is not in a drain that will announce itself in the next flood. A product without a buyer is still better than litter. When a buyer appears, you already have volume.

In a proof photo, show the tied sack or a contained bundle, not a carpet of bags laid out to look abundant. Abundance that can blow away is not a sort. It is a before picture of litter.`,
