    icon: "public",
    status: "ACTIVE",
    content: `A bottle on a Mombasa beach and a bottle in a Nairobi gully are not two problems. They are one item at two mileposts. Inland, the drain is the first mile. The river is the second. The creek and the tide are the later ones. If you only clean the beach, you are sweeping a road that is still being fed from uphill.

That is why household sorting in Nairobi, Kisumu, Eldoret, or a market town is coastal work. You never see the tide. You still decide whether the bottle enters the road. People at the coast already know what arrives. People inland still decide what leaves.

Wind and water change the mix. Film travels farther and snags higher. Bottles float and gather in bends. Heavy HDPE cans sit in mud until a flood moves them. A cleanup that only picks bottles will leave the film in the trees for the next season. A household that only sorts bottles and lets bags out the door is feeding that tree.

You do not need a beach day to be part of the coast. You need the two piles and the film sack. If you do go to the ocean, do not treat the sand as a bin for the picnic. Bring the empties back to the same piles you use at home. A holiday should not undo a weekday habit.

The honest story is not that Kenya’s coast is dirty and the highlands are clean. The honest story is a single chain of household plastic, interrupted wherever a person decides to keep grades apart. Be that interruption.`,
