    icon: "pedal_bike",
    status: "ACTIVE",
    content: `Before any app existed, someone on your street could tell PET from a jerry can in the dark. They learned it because their week depended on it. They have been insulted as “scavengers” by people who throw mixed bags at them and then say recycling has no future. The skill was always here. The missing piece was households doing the first sort so the cart is not a rescue mission.

Talk to a collector like a supplier, not like a donor. Ask what they are buying this week. Ask whether they want caps off. Ask whether they take trays. Then produce that, not a motivational pile. They will come back to a house that is predictable. They will skip a house that is a lucky dip.

This is also why a proof photo that looks like a dump is a fail even if the person “collected a lot.” Volume without grade is the old insult in a new frame. Grade without a speech is respect.

If you have never sold a sack, stand at a yard for ten minutes. Watch what gets weighed and what gets pushed aside. That education is free and it will change how you pack the balcony. You will stop hiding film in bottles. You will stop including the motor-oil can. You will start rinsing because you saw someone else lose a sale over a smell.

PlastyPesa did not invent sorting. It asks a household to join a trade that is already Kenyan. The weekly board is how we recognise the people who show up. The collector is how the plastic actually moves. Keep both in the same picture in your head, and the habit makes sense on a tired evening.`,
