    _id: "learn-ke-20260816-21-turn-it-over",
    title: "Turn it over. The triangle is the truth.",
    description:
      "Brand, colour, and shape all lie. The small moulded triangle is the only mark that was made to be a sort.",
    icon: "rotate_right",
    status: "ACTIVE",
    content: `Every week someone sorts by the story on the label. “It is a water brand, so it is PET.” Sometimes that is true. Sometimes the same brand ships a sports bottle in a different plastic. Sometimes a detergent copies the shape of a juice bottle. The label is advertising. The triangle is manufacturing.

Make a ritual of it. Finish the contents. Rinse. Turn the item over before it enters a pile. If you see a 1 with PET or PETE, it goes with the crackly bottles. If you see a 2 with HDPE, it goes with the jugs and cans. If you see a 5 with PP, it goes with the cups. If you see nothing, you do not invent a number to feel finished. Residual is allowed. Fiction is not.

Teach anyone who shares your sink. Children like the hunt. Adults like not being wrong at the yard. The ritual takes a second and saves a sack. It is also what the harder quiz questions are about: a black tray that is still PET, a bottle that looks like glass and is not, a can that looks like PET and is not.

In a proof photo, if the grade is not obvious from the wall, include the base in the frame or take a second picture that shows the mark. You are not decorating. You are showing your work. A reviewer who can see the triangle does not have to guess your kitchen lighting.

Do this for a month and you will stop seeing “plastic” as one thing. You will see a shop shelf the way a collector sees a cart: products waiting to be kept apart. That change of eye is the whole education. The piles are just where the eye puts its hands.`,
